WEBVTT

00:00:04.598 --> 00:00:06.255
A billing clerk clicks Print.

00:00:11.412 --> 00:00:16.518
A hospital system asked Open Reports for a branded invoice, and got a print ready PDF

00:00:16.518 --> 00:00:21.684
back. By the end of this film, you will have built that invoice yourself, from a blank

00:00:21.684 --> 00:00:24.447
page, and wired it into two real applications.

00:00:34.329 --> 00:00:37.644
Every report in Open Reports has three ingredients.

00:00:37.644 --> 00:00:41.805
A report definition, which is a JSON file describing the layout.

00:00:41.805 --> 00:00:47.201
Your data, which your application already has. And the engine, which takes both and

00:00:47.201 --> 00:00:50.646
returns a PDF, or Word, Excel, or label printer code.

00:00:52.351 --> 00:00:57.242
We will build the definition in the designer, then use it from a hospital system, and from

00:00:57.242 --> 00:01:02.024
a fintech product. Everything you see is real. The companies and patients are fictional.

00:01:07.614 --> 00:01:12.447
This is Open Reports. It runs in the browser, so there is nothing to install.

00:01:12.447 --> 00:01:14.706
We will start with an empty A4 page.

00:01:17.809 --> 00:01:22.994
First, the data. CareDesk will send two things with every invoice: the bill, and the

00:01:22.994 --> 00:01:28.178
branch's branding. We paste a sample of each, so the designer knows the field names.

00:01:33.236 --> 00:01:38.331
The invoice sample has the patient, the visit, every service line, and the amounts.

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:49.344
The branding sample has the hospital name, address, and logo links.

00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:56.841
Amounts should print in rupees, so we set the report's locale and currency once.

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:01.965
Now the layout. A page header repeats at the top of every page.

00:02:01.965 --> 00:02:04.051
We add the hospital logo and name.

00:02:22.223 --> 00:02:27.131
The body holds this one bill. Instead of typing a name, we bind fields from the data.

00:02:27.131 --> 00:02:30.943
When CareDesk sends another bill, these show that patient instead.

00:02:44.304 --> 00:02:47.520
The services table repeats once for every line on the bill.

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:51.990
We point it at the items, and let the designer create the columns from the sample.

00:02:53.207 --> 00:02:57.665
Totals go in a report footer, which appears once, after the last line.

00:02:57.665 --> 00:03:01.740
Each total is a small formula that formats the amount as rupees.

00:03:25.603 --> 00:03:31.410
Last, a page footer with page numbers. It repeats on every page, so a long hospital stay

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:34.709
still prints page one of two, and page two of two.

00:03:40.008 --> 00:03:45.178
Preview runs the real renderer. This is the actual PDF, with the same fonts and page

00:03:45.178 --> 00:03:46.717
breaks CareDesk will get.

00:03:53.971 --> 00:03:57.149
Hospitals print many documents with the same letterhead.

00:03:57.149 --> 00:04:02.257
Instead of rebuilding it in every report, keep it in one file and reuse it as a subreport.

00:04:03.577 --> 00:04:08.917
First, remove the header we drew by hand. Select the logo, press Shift and Enter to select

00:04:08.917 --> 00:04:11.171
the row around it, and delete the row.

00:04:17.222 --> 00:04:22.534
Now add a subreport, and choose the letterhead file from the hospital's report library.

00:04:22.534 --> 00:04:26.747
It holds the logo, the name, the address, and the accreditation seal.

00:04:28.152 --> 00:04:33.257
The letterhead has its own sample branding. We feed it this invoice's branding instead, so

00:04:33.257 --> 00:04:35.583
each branch prints its own name and logo.

00:04:36.805 --> 00:04:41.815
The file is now attached inside the invoice. It travels with the report, so nothing else

00:04:41.815 --> 00:04:43.864
needs to be installed on the server.

00:04:45.088 --> 00:04:49.931
Preview again. The full letterhead is at the top of the page, and it will repeat on every

00:04:49.931 --> 00:04:51.019
page of a long bill.

00:04:57.160 --> 00:05:02.847
Reports are plain JSON files. You can keep them in Git, share them, or hand them to your

00:05:02.847 --> 00:05:03.622
application.

00:05:04.809 --> 00:05:07.128
Export the report file from the menu.

00:05:09.531 --> 00:05:14.067
It is saved in Downloads, as hospital invoice dot report dot JSON.

00:05:15.372 --> 00:05:20.062
To prove the file is complete, go back Home and open it again from the file browser.

00:05:21.332 --> 00:05:23.176
Choose the file we just saved.

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:29.081
Open Reports checks before replacing work that only exists in this browser.

00:05:29.081 --> 00:05:31.045
We have the file, so replace it.

00:05:32.907 --> 00:05:37.860
Everything came back: the data samples, the letterhead subreport, the table, and the

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:43.167
totals. Opening a file does not change anything on the server until you choose to save it.

00:05:49.549 --> 00:05:55.071
This is CareDesk, a hospital information system. It is a separate application, with its

00:05:55.071 --> 00:05:58.499
own billing screens, its own data, and its own server.

00:05:59.941 --> 00:06:05.100
CareDesk keeps its invoice layout as a file in its own report folder, one per branch.

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:07.892
We copy the file we exported into that folder.

00:06:10.637 --> 00:06:14.650
Here is today's billing list. Open Arjun Mehta's cardiology bill.

00:06:16.166 --> 00:06:21.020
Before clicking anything, let's see what CareDesk's server does when someone presses

00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:22.811
Print. Open the developer view.

00:06:24.249 --> 00:06:29.760
This is the real code that runs. Step one: CareDesk loads the bill, and checks this user

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:34.394
may see it. Step two: it reads its own report file from its report folder.

00:06:36.010 --> 00:06:41.892
Step three: it sends the report and the data to Open Reports, with one request, to slash A

00:06:41.892 --> 00:06:46.335
P I slash v one slash render. Open Reports never sees the file path.

00:06:46.335 --> 00:06:49.538
It only receives the report itself, and the data.

00:06:50.850 --> 00:06:55.450
Step four: the PDF comes back, and CareDesk hands it to the browser.

00:06:57.942 --> 00:07:01.476
Now click Download PDF, and watch each step happen.

00:07:04.709 --> 00:07:09.996
The browser asked CareDesk. CareDesk read its report file, and called Open Reports.

00:07:09.996 --> 00:07:15.283
Open Reports rendered the PDF in a fraction of a second, and CareDesk sent it back.

00:07:15.283 --> 00:07:18.850
The file is saved, and here is exactly what it contains.

00:07:22.992 --> 00:07:28.415
The developer view keeps the real request. Here is the body CareDesk sent: the report, the

00:07:28.415 --> 00:07:32.513
format, and the bill's data. Nothing in it is invented for the film.

00:07:33.855 --> 00:07:39.427
Print works the same way. CareDesk gets the PDF, shows it, and hands it to the browser's

00:07:39.427 --> 00:07:40.250
print dialog.

00:07:44.105 --> 00:07:49.454
A long stay is the real test. Rohan Desai's fourteen day admission has forty two lines.

00:07:51.038 --> 00:07:56.743
It runs onto two pages. The letterhead repeats, the page numbers update, and the totals

00:07:56.743 --> 00:07:58.906
appear once, after the last line.

00:08:04.578 --> 00:08:09.449
Northstar has a children's hospital in Mysuru. Switch CareDesk to that branch.

00:08:10.667 --> 00:08:12.599
Open a bill, and download it.

00:08:18.384 --> 00:08:23.843
The report file is identical. CareDesk simply sent this branch's branding as data, so the

00:08:23.843 --> 00:08:28.996
shared letterhead shows the children's hospital name, its address, and its own logo.

00:08:37.564 --> 00:08:42.012
Billing teams change invoices all the time: a new note, a different footer.

00:08:42.012 --> 00:08:45.393
CareDesk lets them do it without leaving the application.

00:08:45.393 --> 00:08:47.054
Click Design invoice layout.

00:08:48.405 --> 00:08:53.531
This is the real Open Reports designer, running in a frame inside CareDesk's own page.

00:08:53.531 --> 00:08:57.703
It opened CareDesk's invoice file, with a sample bill for the preview.

00:08:58.974 --> 00:09:02.279
Embedding it takes one call. Here is CareDesk's code.

00:09:03.596 --> 00:09:08.923
Create designer gets the Open Reports address, CareDesk's report, and the preview data.

00:09:08.923 --> 00:09:14.004
Save mode host means edits come back to CareDesk. Open Reports does not store them.

00:09:14.004 --> 00:09:18.107
The save function sends the edited report to CareDesk's own server.

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:26.282
Let's make a change. Select the balance due line.

00:09:27.527 --> 00:09:29.441
Change it to read amount payable.

00:09:32.095 --> 00:09:36.778
Now save. To be safe, let's see what happens if CareDesk's storage is down.

00:09:36.778 --> 00:09:39.776
We switched on a test failure for this one save.

00:09:41.193 --> 00:09:46.583
The save failed, and the designer says so. The edit is still there, nothing is lost.

00:09:46.583 --> 00:09:47.289
Save again.

00:09:48.555 --> 00:09:53.745
Saved. The developer view shows CareDesk checking the report with Open Reports, and then

00:09:53.745 --> 00:09:56.162
writing version two to its report folder.

00:09:58.563 --> 00:10:00.541
Back to the bill, and print again.

00:10:07.502 --> 00:10:12.713
The printed invoice now says amount payable. The edit, the save, and the next print all

00:10:12.713 --> 00:10:15.049
used CareDesk's own copy of the report.

00:10:22.759 --> 00:10:26.269
Ledgerline is a payments dashboard for small businesses.

00:10:26.269 --> 00:10:31.785
It uses Open Reports differently. Instead of keeping report files itself, it stores them

00:10:31.785 --> 00:10:34.606
in Open Reports once, as published templates.

00:10:35.907 --> 00:10:40.011
Publishing is one call per template, usually part of a deploy.

00:10:40.011 --> 00:10:42.327
Here it is, with the real requests.

00:10:44.177 --> 00:10:49.103
Each template now has a published version. Published versions never change, so every

00:10:49.103 --> 00:10:52.035
invoice uses exactly the layout that was reviewed.

00:10:53.417 --> 00:10:59.391
On the invoices page, Download PDF sends only the template ID, the format, and the data.

00:10:59.391 --> 00:11:02.514
No report definition travels with the request.

00:11:03.953 --> 00:11:09.174
Same flow as before: the browser asks Ledgerline, Ledgerline asks Open Reports for

00:11:09.174 --> 00:11:13.058
template ledgerline merchant invoice, and the PDF comes back.

00:11:15.636 --> 00:11:19.235
For screens where people read reports, there is a drop-in viewer.

00:11:19.235 --> 00:11:24.217
The statements page is one viewer element, given the template ID and this merchant's data.

00:11:25.527 --> 00:11:30.110
It comes with search, print, and downloads. Find one transaction.

00:11:31.388 --> 00:11:35.203
And the merchant can take the whole month to Excel, for their accountant.

00:11:38.877 --> 00:11:44.252
Five ways to use it. Your application keeps the report file, or keeps one per customer,

00:11:44.252 --> 00:11:49.565
and sends it with the data. Or Open Reports stores published templates, and you render

00:11:49.565 --> 00:11:50.245
them by ID.

00:11:53.083 --> 00:11:58.000
Drop in the viewer for reading screens. And embed the designer, so your own users can

00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:02.108
change their documents, while your application keeps control of saving.

00:12:05.330 --> 00:12:10.598
Open Reports is free and open source. Try the live demo, read the integration guide, and

00:12:10.598 --> 00:12:15.806
if it saves you a week of report work, a star on GitHub helps other developers find it.

00:12:15.806 --> 00:12:17.003
Thanks for watching.
